Break test — `make contrast-check`, the gate under ADR-0047 D6
Phase 20e.1, 2026-08-09. Run in the chat session's own clone of origin/main,
on the two commits that introduce the palette tokens and the gate.

A gate that has only ever been seen GREEN is indistinguishable from a gate that
cannot fail. Seven breaks, each aimed at a different claim, with a green control
either side of the whole run:

  1  the thing it exists for      a state put back under the floor -> RED
  2  drift nobody chose           --ok lightened; no state colour edited -> RED
  3  THE INSTRUMENT               the discovery's own defect, replanted:
                                  color(srgb x y z) divided by 255 -> REFUSAL
  4  zero states                  an empty contract -> REFUSAL, not a clean table
  5  no contract at all           -> REFUSAL
  6  a probe that is not a colour -> REFUSAL, never a skip
  7  the built page missing       -> REFUSAL, not nothing-to-check

Break 3 is the one that matters most and it is not about contrast at all. The
first contrast measurement this project took was WRONG and looked like a
finding: color-mix() resolves to color(srgb 0.44 0.62 0.90), the parser treated
those components as 0..255, and six different colours came back at 20.92:1. That
defect is replanted here verbatim, and the control catches it before a single
state is printed - 1.01 instead of 21.00, refusal, no verdict. An instrument has
to be trusted before its readings mean anything.

Break 2 is the failure mode the gate was actually built for. Nobody edits a
state colour to be fainter; a shared token moves and every mix toward --line
follows it quietly. Here --ok alone is lightened and `done` falls from 3.37 to
1.68 without its own definition being touched.

The exit statuses below are taken directly from `$?` after the command, NOT
through a pipe: on 2026-07-28 a Checkov break test read grep's status instead of
Checkov's and looked exactly like a gate that would not break.

The sha256 prefix of every input is printed on each run, because on 2026-08-08
four one-clause defects each reddened the same fixture and a later run of the
identical loop reported all four green - CPython had validated a cached .pyc on
(mtime in whole seconds, size), and every variant was byte-identical in size.
Node compiles from source on every process here, but the reading is recorded
rather than assumed: the hashes below differ per break, and the two greens share
the committed one.

